Give the device a memory type that is only device-local.

KosmicKrisp has one memory type, host-visible and device-local. Under venus
every host-visible allocation is a pointer the renderer imports
(VK_EXT_external_memory_host), a plain MTLBuffer with no heap, so every image
the guest binds is backed by a linear texture
(buffer-backed-optimal-textures.patch). A linear texture is slower to sample
and to render into than a tiled one, and the guest's glass captures, blurs and
glyph atlas are read many times a frame.

The second type is the same heap without HOST_VISIBLE. venus does not import a
pointer for memory the guest cannot map, so the renderer allocates it through
kk_alloc_bo, a MTLHeap, and an optimal image bound to it keeps its tiled
layout. It comes after the existing type, so a caller taking the first type
that fits its flags gets what it got before; one that wants tiled images asks
for device-local memory that is not host-visible.

--- a/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_physical_device.c
+++ b/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_physical_device.c
@@ -1192,6 +1192,15 @@
       .heapIndex = sysmem_heap_idx,
    };
 
+   /* The same heap, for memory nothing maps. A renderer that backs
+    * host-visible memory with a pointer it imports allocates this kind itself,
+    * so it is a MTLHeap and an optimal image bound to it is tiled.
+    */
+   pdev->mem_types[pdev->mem_type_count++] = (VkMemoryType){
+      .propertyFlags = VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_DEVICE_LOCAL_BIT,
+      .heapIndex = sysmem_heap_idx,
+   };
+
    assert(pdev->mem_heap_count <= ARRAY_SIZE(pdev->mem_heaps));
    assert(pdev->mem_type_count <= ARRAY_SIZE(pdev->mem_types));
 
